/-!
# The Nisnevich topology on schemes, via cd-structures

This module is the schemes-level half of Voevodsky's cd-structure program:
it defines the **Nisnevich distinguished squares** on the category of
schemes (`IsNisnevichSquare`), the **Nisnevich cd-structure**
(`nisnevichCdStructure`), and the **Nisnevich topology** (`nisnevichTopology`)
as the Grothendieck topology generated by the distinguished squares
(`GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure`, from
`BoringMath.CategoryTheory.Sites.CdStructure`).

A commutative square `sq` in `Scheme` is a Nisnevich distinguished square
when it is cartesian, `sq.f₂₄` is an open immersion, `sq.f₃₄` is étale, and
`sq.f₃₄` is an isomorphism over the open complement of the image of
`sq.f₂₄` — stated scheme-theoretically as: the fiber of `sq.f₃₄` over each
point `x` outside the image of `sq.f₂₄` maps isomorphically to `Spec κ(x)`.

The point-set criterion `nisnevichSquare_iff` is stated as a corollary of
the definition, never as the foundation; the topology agreement
`nisnevichTopology_eq_ofCdStructure` routes through
`GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure` as landed in the B1 module.

## References
* V. Voevodsky, *Homotopy theory of simplicial sheaves in completely
  decomposable topologies*, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), 1399–1406,
  §2 (the upper, or Nisnevich, cd-structure) and Proposition 2.16 (the
  associated topology is the Nisnevich topology), arXiv:0805.4576.
* V. Voevodsky, *Homology of schemes*, Selecta Math. (N.S.) 2 (1996),
  111–153 (cd-topologies on categories of schemes).
* C. Mazza, V. Voevodsky, C. Weibel, *Lecture Notes on Motivic Cohomology*,
  Clay Mathematics Monographs 2 (2006), Part 3 (Nisnevich sheaves with
  transfers, Lectures 11–13).
  REVIEW(citation): pin the exact lecture defining the Nisnevich topology.
* Y. Nisnevich, *The completely decomposed topology on schemes and
  associated descent spectral sequences in algebraic K-theory*, in:
  Algebraic K-theory: connections with geometry and topology (Lake
  Louise, AB, 1987), NATO ASI Ser. C 279, Kluwer, 1989, 241–342.
* J. Riou, `Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.MayerVietorisSquare` (the docstring
  names Nisnevich distinguished squares as its motivating example).
-/

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

open CategoryTheory

-- Reference: V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), §2, the upper
-- (Nisnevich) cd-structure: a distinguished square is a pullback square with
-- `p` étale, `e` an open embedding and `p⁻¹(X ∖ e(A)) → X ∖ e(A)` an
-- isomorphism (arXiv:0805.4576); cf. J. Riou, `MayerVietorisSquare` docstring.
/-- A **Nisnevich distinguished square** in the category of schemes: a
cartesian square whose right leg `f₂₄` is an open immersion, whose bottom
leg `f₃₄` is étale, and whose bottom leg is an isomorphism over the open
complement of the image of the right leg — stated scheme-theoretically:
the fiber of `f₃₄` over each point `x` outside `Set.range f₂₄` maps
isomorphically to `Spec κ(x)`. -/
structure IsNisnevichSquare (sq : Square Scheme.{u}) : Prop where
  /-- The square is cartesian. -/
  isPullback : sq.IsPullback
  /-- The right leg is an open immersion. -/
  isOpenImmersion_f₂₄ : IsOpenImmersion sq.f₂₄
  /-- The bottom leg is étale. -/
  etale_f₃₄ : Etale sq.f₃₄
  /-- The bottom leg is an isomorphism over the open complement of the
  image of the right leg. -/
  isoOnComplement : ∀ x : sq.X₄, x ∉ Set.range sq.f₂₄ →
    IsIso (sq.f₃₄.fiberToSpecResidueField x)

-- Reference: V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), §2 (the upper
-- cd-structure on the category of schemes is the set of Nisnevich
-- distinguished squares), arXiv:0805.4576.
/-- The **Nisnevich cd-structure** on the category of schemes: the set of
Nisnevich distinguished squares. -/
def nisnevichCdStructure : CdStructure Scheme.{u} where
  squares := {sq : Square Scheme.{u} | IsNisnevichSquare sq}

-- Reference: V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), Proposition 2.16
-- (the topology associated with the upper cd-structure is the Nisnevich
-- topology), arXiv:0805.4576; `GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure` (B1
-- module, `BoringMath/CategoryTheory/Sites/CdStructure.lean`).
/-- The **Nisnevich topology** on the category of schemes: the Grothendieck
topology generated by the Nisnevich cd-structure. -/
def nisnevichTopology : GrothendieckTopology Scheme.{u} :=
  GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure nisnevichCdStructure

-- Reference: `GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure` (B1 module,
-- `BoringMath/CategoryTheory/Sites/CdStructure.lean`); V. Voevodsky,
-- J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), Proposition 2.16, arXiv:0805.4576.
/-- **Topology agreement**: the Nisnevich topology is the Grothendieck
topology generated by the Nisnevich cd-structure. This is a primitive
definitional lemma (the `rfl` is permitted here by `docs/STYLE.md` §5); the
independent point-set definition of the Nisnevich topology and its
agreement with this cd-topology is the Tier-2 target. -/
theorem nisnevichTopology_eq_ofCdStructure :
    nisnevichTopology = GrothendieckTopology.ofCdStructure nisnevichCdStructure := rfl

-- Reference: definition of `nisnevichCdStructure` (V. Voevodsky, J. Pure
-- Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), §2, arXiv:0805.4576).
/-- Membership in the Nisnevich cd-structure is exactly being a Nisnevich
distinguished square. -/
theorem mem_nisnevichCdStructure_squares_iff (sq : Square Scheme.{u}) :
    sq ∈ nisnevichCdStructure.squares ↔ IsNisnevichSquare sq :=
  ⟨fun h => h, fun h => h⟩

-- Reference: V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), §2,
-- arXiv:0805.4576; the point-set criterion of Y. Nisnevich, NATO ASI
-- Ser. C 279 (1989), 241–342.
/-- **Point-set criterion for Nisnevich distinguished squares**, as a
corollary of the definition (never the foundation): a square of schemes is
Nisnevich distinguished exactly when it is cartesian, its right leg is an
open immersion, its bottom leg is étale, and the bottom leg is an
isomorphism over every point of the open complement of the image of the
right leg. -/
theorem nisnevichSquare_iff (sq : Square Scheme.{u}) :
    IsNisnevichSquare sq ↔
      sq.IsPullback ∧ IsOpenImmersion sq.f₂₄ ∧ Etale sq.f₃₄ ∧
        ∀ x : sq.X₄, x ∉ Set.range sq.f₂₄ →
          IsIso (sq.f₃₄.fiberToSpecResidueField x) :=
  ⟨fun h => ⟨h.isPullback, h.isOpenImmersion_f₂₄, h.etale_f₃₄, h.isoOnComplement⟩,
    fun h => ⟨h.1, h.2.1, h.2.2.1, h.2.2.2⟩⟩

-- Reference: `GrothendieckTopology.sieve_ofTwoArrows_mem_of_mem_squares`
-- (B1 module, `BoringMath/CategoryTheory/Sites/CdStructure.lean`);
-- V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), §2, arXiv:0805.4576 (the
-- two-arrow sieve of a distinguished square covers its lower right corner).
/-- **Generation clause at the schemes level**: the two-arrow sieve of a
Nisnevich distinguished square covers its lower right corner for the
Nisnevich topology. -/
theorem sieve_ofTwoArrows_mem_nisnevichTopology (sq : Square Scheme.{u})
    (h : IsNisnevichSquare sq) :
    Sieve.ofTwoArrows sq.f₂₄ sq.f₃₄ ∈ nisnevichTopology sq.X₄ :=
  GrothendieckTopology.sieve_ofTwoArrows_mem_of_mem_squares nisnevichCdStructure
    ((mem_nisnevichCdStructure_squares_iff sq).mpr h)

-- Reference: V. Voevodsky, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 214 (2010), Proposition
-- 2.16, arXiv:0805.4576 (the Nisnevich topology contains the Zariski
-- topology); C. Mazza, V. Voevodsky, C. Weibel, *Lecture Notes on Motivic
-- Cohomology*, Clay Math. Monographs 2 (2006), Part 3.
-- REVIEW(citation): pin the exact lecture in MVW.
/-- The Zariski topology is contained in the Nisnevich topology. -/
